The setup: $q_n$ has signature $(n,2)$, so $G_n\cong\operatorname{SO}(n,2)^0$. The vector $e_{n+2}$ has $q_n(e_{n+2})=-1$, so its orthogonal complement has signature $(n,1)$ and $H_n\cong\operatorname{SO}(n,1)^0$. The question therefore asks for a cocompact lattice in $\operatorname{SO}(n,1)$ that deforms, through discrete Zariski-dense, infinite-covolume, ergodic representations, into $\operatorname{SO}(n,2)$. This matches the rigidity/flexibility theory of Bader–Fisher–Miller–Stover, which I could not reconstruct in enough detail to pin down the threshold dimension. The value $n=3$ is my best guess, not a derived result.

